<div id="p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<br class="ltx_break ltx_centering">
</div>
<div id="p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="p4.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:144%;">Supplementary Material 
<br class="ltx_break"></span></p>
</div>
<section id="A1" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix A </span>Inference for Arbitrary Resolution</h2>

<div id="A1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Diffusion models typically face scalability issues when dealing with high-resolution images, often yielding inferior results while incurring significantly increased computational costs. Consequently, existing diffusion-based codecs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">37</a>, <a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>, <a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">40</a>]</cite> primarily target small images with resolutions around 512×512 or resized images. To enhance the practicality of StableCodec, we adopt a tiled VAE approach <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite> to split high-resolution images into tiles and process them sequentially in both the VAE encoder and decoder. For one-step denoising, we employ a similar latent aggregation strategy <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">31</a>, <a href="#bib.bib63" title="" class="ltx_ref">63</a>]</cite>, which processes latent patches individually and aggregates overlapping pixels using a Gaussian weight map. These methods enable StableCodec to support arbitrary-resolution inference with memory consumption under 9 GB, greatly improving its efficiency and practicality for real-world deployment.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">However, we observe that StableCodec sometimes produces color shifts when reconstructing high-resolution images, as illustrated in Fig. <a href="#A1.F8" title="Figure 8 ‣ Appendix A Inference for Arbitrary Resolution ‣ StableCodec: Taming One-Step Diffusion for Extreme Image Compression" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>. This issue has also been noted in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib63" title="" class="ltx_ref">63</a>, <a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite>. To address this, we apply a quantized version of adaptive instance normalization <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib63" title="" class="ltx_ref">63</a>]</cite> on the reconstructed high-resolution image <math id="A1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{x}</annotation></semantics></math>, aligning its mean (<math id="A1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{\hat{x}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>μ</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{\hat{x}}</annotation></semantics></math>) and variance (<math id="A1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{\hat{x}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{\hat{x}}</annotation></semantics></math>) with those of the original image (<math id="A1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{x}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{x}</annotation></semantics></math>):</p>
<table id="A1.E9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.E9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{x}^{c}=\frac{\hat{x}-\mu_{\hat{x}}}{\sigma_{\hat{x}}}\cdot\hat{\sigma_{x}}+\hat{\mu_{x}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>c</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></msub></mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></msub></mfrac><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mover accent="true"><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow><mo>+</mo><mover accent="true"><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{x}^{c}=\frac{\hat{x}-\mu_{\hat{x}}}{\sigma_{\hat{x}}}\cdot\hat{\sigma_{x}}+\hat{\mu_{x}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(9)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mu_{x}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mu_{x}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\sigma_{x}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\sigma_{x}}</annotation></semantics></math> are 16-bit-quantized from <math id="A1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{x}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{x}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="A1.EGx3" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A1.E10"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.E10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\hat{\mu_{x}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\hat{\mu_{x}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.E10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\frac{\lfloor\mu_{x}\cdot(2^{16}-1)+2^{-1}\rfloor}{2^{16}-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mn>16</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌋</mo></mrow><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mn>16</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\frac{\lfloor\mu_{x}\cdot(2^{16}-1)+2^{-1}\rfloor}{2^{16}-1}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(10)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A1.E11"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.E11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\hat{\sigma_{x}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\hat{\sigma_{x}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.E11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\frac{\lfloor\sigma_{x}\cdot(2^{16}-1)+2^{-1}\rfloor}{2^{16}-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mn>16</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌋</mo></mrow><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mn>16</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\frac{\lfloor\sigma_{x}\cdot(2^{16}-1)+2^{-1}\rfloor}{2^{16}-1}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(11)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.p2.3" class="ltx_p">Here, <math id="A1.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{x}^{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>c</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{x}^{c}</annotation></semantics></math> represents the color-corrected reconstruction, and <math id="A1.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{x}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{x}</annotation></semantics></math> contain the mean and variance values for the RGB channels, each represented as 32-bit floating point values. We find that quantizing these values to 16 bits does not significantly affect correction performance. This strategy effectively refines the color of high-resolution reconstructions with only a minimal increase in bit cost (96 bits per image), as demonstrated in Fig. <a href="#A1.F8" title="Figure 8 ‣ Appendix A Inference for Arbitrary Resolution ‣ StableCodec: Taming One-Step Diffusion for Extreme Image Compression" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A1.F8" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2506.21977v1/color.png" id="A1.F8.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:322/337;" width="322" height="337" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A1.F8.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 8</span>: </span><span id="A1.F8.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">Visual examples of color fix<span id="A1.F8.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> from CLIC 2020 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib60" title="" class="ltx_ref">60</a>]</cite>. 16-bit color fix brings clear refinement with negligible bits increase.</span></span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="A2" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix B </span>Network Structure</h2>

<div id="A2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We present our entropy model in Fig. <a href="#A2.F9" title="Figure 9 ‣ Appendix B Network Structure ‣ StableCodec: Taming One-Step Diffusion for Extreme Image Compression" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>, with the detailed network architecture shown in Fig. <a href="#A2.F10" title="Figure 10 ‣ Appendix B Network Structure ‣ StableCodec: Taming One-Step Diffusion for Extreme Image Compression" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a>. Given the quantized latent <math id="A2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{y}</annotation></semantics></math>, the entropy model estimates its distribution for arithmetic coding. Following <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">45</a>]</cite>, our entropy model is built with a hyperprior module and an autoregressive context model, where we first obtain and transmit a hyperprior <math id="A2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi_{hyper}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi_{hyper}</annotation></semantics></math> from <math id="A2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math> using the hyper transform <math id="A2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{a}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{a}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{s}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="A2.E12" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="A2.E12X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.E12X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle z=h_{a}(y),\hat{z}=Q(z),\Phi_{hyper}=h_{s}(\hat{z})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>z</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>z</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle z=h_{a}(y),\hat{z}=Q(z),\Phi_{hyper}=h_{s}(\hat{z})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_right">(12)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Here, <math id="A2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math> has 320 channels with <math id="A2.p1.m7" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="64\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>64</mn><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">64\times</annotation></semantics></math> (a spatial compression ratio of 64), while <math id="A2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>z</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{z}</annotation></semantics></math> have 160 channels with <math id="A2.p1.m10" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="256\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>256</mn><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">256\times</annotation></semantics></math>. To balance the coding performance and efficiency, we construct a 4-step autoregressive process using quadtree partition <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">38</a>]</cite> and latent residual prediction <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">44</a>]</cite>. The detailed autoregressive process to estimate the Gaussian parameters, <math id="A2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math>, for <math id="A2.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{y}</annotation></semantics></math> is illustrated in Fig. <a href="#A2.F9" title="Figure 9 ‣ Appendix B Network Structure ‣ StableCodec: Taming One-Step Diffusion for Extreme Image Compression" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>. Following this, arithmetic coding is applied to encode <math id="A2.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{y}</annotation></semantics></math> into a bitstream, or decode <math id="A2.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{y}</annotation></semantics></math> from the bitstream. For efficient network construction, we primarily rely on modified versions of InceptionNeXt <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib71" title="" class="ltx_ref">71</a>]</cite> and GatedCNN <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib70" title="" class="ltx_ref">70</a>]</cite>, as detailed in Fig. <a href="#A2.F10" title="Figure 10 ‣ Appendix B Network Structure ‣ StableCodec: Taming One-Step Diffusion for Extreme Image Compression" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A2.F9" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2506.21977v1/network1.svg" id="A2.F9.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:685/186;" width="685" height="186"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A2.F9.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 9</span>: </span><span id="A2.F9.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">(Left) Illustration of the entropy model.<span id="A2.F9.6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> We build our entropy model on the basis of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">45</a>]</cite>, which consists of a pair of hyper transforms, <math id="A2.F9.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{a}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{a}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.F9.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{s}</annotation></semantics></math>, and a context model to perform entropy estimation for <math id="A2.F9.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo mathsize="1.110em">^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{y}</annotation></semantics></math> in an autoregressive manner. </span>(Right) Illustration of the 4-step autoregressive process.<span id="A2.F9.6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> We divide <math id="A2.F9.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo mathsize="1.110em">^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{y}</annotation></semantics></math> into 4 groups (<math id="A2.F9.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{y}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo mathsize="1.110em">^</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{y}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A2.F9.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{y}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo mathsize="1.110em">^</mo></mover><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{y}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A2.F9.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{y}_{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo mathsize="1.110em">^</mo></mover><mn>3</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{y}_{3}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.F9.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{y}_{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo mathsize="1.110em">^</mo></mover><mn>4</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{y}_{4}</annotation></semantics></math>) using quadtree partition <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">38</a>]</cite>. For each <math id="A2.F9.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{y}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo mathsize="1.110em">^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{y}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, we estimate its Gaussian parameters, <math id="A2.F9.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.F9.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, with the hyperprior <math id="A2.F9.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi_{hyper}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi_{hyper}</annotation></semantics></math> and previously decoded groups <math id="A2.F9.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{y}_{&lt;i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo mathsize="1.110em">^</mo></mover><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{y}_{&lt;i}</annotation></semantics></math>. The parameter networks contain a shared context model and private adapters. AD represents arithmetic decoding the bitstream of <math id="A2.F9.m30" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{y}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo mathsize="1.110em">^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{y}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> given corresponding Gaussian parameters, <math id="A2.F9.m31" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.F9.m32" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>. Additionally, we incorporate latent residual prediction (LRP) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">44</a>]</cite> to alleviate the quantization error.</span></span></figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="A2.F10" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2506.21977v1/network2.svg" id="A2.F10.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:685/289;" width="685" height="289"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A2.F10.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 10</span>: </span><span id="A2.F10.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">Module structures and network details.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="A2.T6" class="ltx_table">
<table id="A2.T6.3" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_align_middle">
<tr id="A2.T6.3.1" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="A2.T6.3.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_t" rowspan="2"><span id="A2.T6.3.1.1.1" class="ltx_text">Method</span></td>
<td id="A2.T6.3.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_t" colspan="4">Encoding Time (s)</td>
<td id="A2.T6.3.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t" colspan="5">Decoding Time (s)</td></tr>
<tr id="A2.T6.3.2" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="A2.T6.3.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t"><math id="A2.T6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}_{\mathrm{SD}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>SD</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}_{\mathrm{SD}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="A2.T6.3.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t"><math id="A2.T6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}_{\mathrm{Aux}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>Aux</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}_{\mathrm{Aux}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="A2.T6.3.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t"><math id="A2.T6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{a}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{a}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="A2.T6.3.2.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_t">EE</td>
<td id="A2.T6.3.2.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">ED</td>
<td id="A2.T6.3.2.6" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t"><math id="A2.T6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{s}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="A2.T6.3.2.7" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t"><math id="A2.T6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{Aux}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>Aux</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{Aux}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="A2.T6.3.2.8" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t"><math id="A2.T6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon_{\mathrm{SD}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>SD</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon_{\mathrm{SD}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="A2.T6.3.2.9" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t"><math id="A2.T6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{SD}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>SD</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{SD}}</annotation></semantics></math></td></tr>
<tr id="A2.T6.3.3" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="A2.T6.3.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_tt">StableCodec (Ours)</td>
<td id="A2.T6.3.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt">0.108</td>
<td id="A2.T6.3.3.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt">0.014</td>
<td id="A2.T6.3.3.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt">0.005</td>
<td id="A2.T6.3.3.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_tt">0.029</td>
<td id="A2.T6.3.3.6" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt">0.041</td>
<td id="A2.T6.3.3.7" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt">0.004</td>
<td id="A2.T6.3.3.8" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt">0.004</td>
<td id="A2.T6.3.3.9" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt">0.112</td>
<td id="A2.T6.3.3.10" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt">0.161</td></tr>
